nce from scrubs that was created by  Donald Faison it's basically the same  move for move in the game itself so it  seems pretty clear here that the makers  of  tonight are trying to make a profit off  of the dances that were created by other  people but just because the dances are  almost identical does not mean that is  copyright infringement first a little  background on copyright law in general  how do you get copyright protection well  you have to have original expression  that is fixed into a tangible medium  here video counts as a tangible medium  so if you record a dance into video  that's actually one of the preferred  ways of copywriting a potential dance so  that's not really an issue but you  should know that copyright does not  apply to ideas themselves only the  actual expression and you can't  copyright functional things that's what  patent protection is for and courts have  interpreted the copyright Clause of the  Constitution as requiring minimal  creativity in addition to originality  originality is the bedrock principle of  copyright that requires that to qualify  for copyright protection a work must be  original to its author and that means  that that work must be independently  created by the author itself and it must  possess at least some minimal degree of  creativity so for example you can't  copyright a single note or a single word  but you can string a series of those  notes together  to make a copy writable song and you can  string together a series of  uncopyrightable words to form an  original book but there is absolutely no  bright-line rule that says it requires  this many words to form a copyrightable  phrase or a book or it requires this  many notes to be a copyrightable song  certainly there are very short songs and  very short literary works that despite  being very very small still qualify for  copyright expression so it is an open  question as to how many dance moves you  have to string together in order to form  a copy rideable 