t  Act it issued a report along with the  act itself to clarify some of the  changes that it made and in that house  report Congress specified that  choreographic works do not include  social dance steps and simple routines  thus for example Congress exempted  things like the basic waltz step the  hustle step the second position of  classical ballet all of those things are  not copyrightable but Congress also  specified that this is not a restriction  against the incorporation of social  dance steps and symbol routines into an  otherwise larger choreographic work the  Second Circuit which is one step below  the Supreme Court in the Horgan versus  Macmillan case how  that social dance steps folk dance steps  and individual valet steps may be  utilized as the choreographers basic  material much in the same way that words  are the writers basic material so at  issue here are several lawsuits that  were filed by the rapper to Milly  alfonso ribeiro and backpack kid they  all seem to be represented by the same  law firm and they're all filing suit in  the federal court in Los Angeles the  Central District of California where  I've practiced many many times and in  those lawsuits the performers are  alleging that the makers of fortnight  and a number of other video game  developers have basically stolen all of  the different dances to include them in  downloadable content from which they  make a profit and that that qualifies as  copyright infringement they are fairly  bare-bones complaints but they do seem  to state the essential elements for  copyright infringement so given that the  dances in question here are basically  identical the main legal issue is  whether the dances are social dances or  whether they are choreographic works in  other words are these dances  copyrightable in the first place now  normally I would just look up the case  law and look for cases that have  interpreted the phrase social dances in  the Copyright Act and compare them to  cases that have interpreted  choreographic works and give you an easy  answer as to whether the dances in  question qualify as social dances or not  unfortunately there are very very few  cases that have dealt with this language  again this is a very new aspect of the  copyright law in general and really very  few people have even litigated this  there's basically one case on point and  that again comes out of the Central  District of California which is where a  lot of copyright law originates because  that's where Hollywood is basically the  only court case to deal with this  language is the court case of Bikram  yoga versus evolution yoga which dealt  with a sequence of 26 yoga poses and the  issue there was whether the yoga poses  would qualify as a copyright of a work a  choreographic work or whether they were  non copyrightable in the first place the  court sided with the defendants stating  that the 26 yoga poses quote hardly  seems to fall within the definition of  pantomime or choreographic work because  of the simplicity of the sequence and  the fact that it is not a dramatic  formitz however the plaintiff appealed  to the Ninth Circuit and while the Ninth  Circuit agreed that the defendants  should win they did not agree on the  basis for that decision and the Ninth  Circuit held that the sequence of yoga  moves was not copyrightable not because  it wasn't a choreographic work but  because it was a demonstration of an  idea process or system to which  copyright protection may in no case  extend so given that this is a matter of  first impression where the courts really  haven't weighed in on what constitutes  copyright infringement in the context of  a dance or a choreography who's going to  win these lawsuits well let's think like  a lawyer so here's what I think both  sides are going to argue first the  plaintiffs are going to argue that these  dances are very very unique they're  instantly recognizable if you see  someone performing the Carlton dance you  know that that is the Carlton dance that  appeared on The Fresh Prince of bel-air  and the fact that they are so unique and  so recognizable shows that they should  get copyright protection despite the  fact that they are very very short and  just in the same way that you can string  together uncopyrightable notes to form a  copyrightable song you can string  together uncopyrightable moves movement  in the air to form a copyrightable dance  and it would be weird to make dance less  copyrightable than its counterparts in  the literary or musical world I think  that the defendants would counter by  saying that these are not minimally  creative or unique these are just  standard dance moves that you'll see  anywhere like a scene affair or a trope  that they're just putting together what  people have been doing for a very very  long time and most important I think the  defendants would argue that the dance is  in question are social dances and  therefore not copyrightable they are far  more akin to the foxtrot  or the waltz than they are to an entire  hour-long ballet a work of choreography  that was intended to receive copyright  protection in my own personal view as  someone who has litigated many many  copyright issues I think the defendants  have the better argument here to me if  the phrase social dance the exception to  the copyright clause is meant to have  any meaning whatsoever I think it's  meant to include the dances at  issue here I think that Congress  intended to create a differentiation  between large choreographed work and  just a social dance like the waltz  maybe the Macarena would qualify as well  and I think these short dances would  qualify as social dances that were meant  to be performed in public as opposed to  being a copyrighted work after all we  don't want copyright law to allow  artists to sue anyone performing their  dance moves in public  wherever the young people are performing  their dance moves clubs sock hops I  don't know wherever you people hang out  so this is untested and could go either  way but I think that the defendants are  likely to win as a matter of law but  because this is a matter of first  impression the courts really haven't  weighed in on this and there are  reasonable arguments on either side the  courts could go either way the law is  constantly evolving and I'll be  fascinated to see how this one turns out  and if you want to create your own  copyrightable choreography first you  need to learn how to dance I would  recommend mi rhoi Barnardo's Skillshare  classes on basic hip-hop dancing where  you'll learn the basics of modern dance  and how to dance to people like Justin  Timberlake and Drake to artists who have  never sued your favorite game developer  Skillshare is an online learning  community that has over 20,000 classes  on everything like lifestyle design and  technology the first 500 legal eagles to  click on the link below will get to  three months of Skillshare the free  premium membership gives you unlimited  access to must know topics so you can  improve your skills and learn new things  like learning how to dance better than I  can so click the link get to three  months of skill share and start learning  today so until next time I'll see you in  court

